course: dictionary
lesson: 4246
title: One word, many meanings: grant
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word grant has more than one meaning, so you need its context to know which one fits. Look at the words around it and ask what would make sense.
explain: «Genies grant wishes in fairy tales» means the genie gives the wish someone asked for. Here grant means to give or allow.
explain: «I grant that it's hard» means I agree it is true that it is hard. Granted, in «Granted, it's late», works the same way: it means you admit something before you go on.
explain: «The school got a grant for new computers» is different. A grant is money given for a special purpose, and it does not have to be paid back.
fact: Grant can be a verb (grant, granted), a noun (a grant), or a name (Grant).
fact: «To grant» means to give or allow something someone asked for, such as «grant a wish».
fact: «To grant that» means to agree that something is true, such as «I grant that it's hard».
fact: «A grant» is money given by a government or organisation for a special purpose, which does not have to be paid back.
fact: «To take something for granted» means to expect it without thanking or noticing it.
exercise: «Genies grant wishes in fairy tales.» What does grant mean here? | To buy something in a shop | To lose something | *To give what someone asked for | To hide a secret
exercise: «I grant that it's hard, but keep trying.» What does grant mean here? | To forget something | To give a prize only to winners | To put something in a box | *To agree that something is true
exercise: «The school got a grant for new computers.» What is a grant here? | *Money given for a special purpose, which does not have to be paid back | A type of computer | A long list of rules | Money given for a special purpose, not paid back
test: In «Peter and Rosemary Grant measured finch beaks», what is Grant? | A kind of bird | *A family name, used for people | Money given for a project | To give or allow
test: Which phrase means you expect something without thanking or noticing it? | Grant a wish | Granted, it is late | *Take for granted | A research grant
exam: «Granted, the test was long, but Mia finished it.» What does granted mean here? | Given away for free | Lost or forgotten | *I admit it is true: the test was long | Written down carefully
